# a-sealed-base — plan 11 F7.
#
# The player closes the perimeter completely, and it does not save
# them: `docs/ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § Sealing the perimeter is punished,
# not forbidden.  Three enemies arrive from three directions and
# besiege THREE different wall hexes, so there is no one place to
# concentrate fire.
#
# ── What makes this one evidence ────────────────────────────────
#
# `count targets` is a SET, and that is the whole point.  The
# implementation this phase exists to avoid — "the nearest wall",
# computed once and handed to everybody — also gives every enemy a
# target, also leaves them all attacking a wall, and also passes
# `enemies passable`.  What it cannot do is produce THREE hexes.  So
# the number below is the measurement, and `count alive 3 3` beside it
# is what stops three targets from being read off one enemy.
#
# It is also the only enemy measurement here that does not care about
# spawn order.  Three markers means round-robin over a hash iteration,
# so `enemy 0` would be asserting something this file cannot repeat;
# a set has no order to get wrong.
#
# ── The world ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   three corridors of grass, twelve hexes each, meeting at the core:
#     east  (12, 0) -> (0, 0)      sealed at (2, 0)
#     west  (-12, 0) -> (0, 0)     sealed at (-2, 0)
#     south (0, 12) -> (0, 0)      sealed at (0, 2)
#
# Each enemy walks its own corridor in and stops one hex short of its
# own seal, which is three different hexes because they came from
# three different places — and nothing coordinates them.

echo a sealed base: three ways in, all shut, and three different bites

do toggle_mode

# The core is where the target is.
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# Three spawns, twelve hexes out — clear of close_spawn_disable_radius
# (10) and well inside the scrambler bubble (25), so the FIELD steers
# from the first tick.
# ⚠ TWICE: the place-kind cycle is spawn -> target -> tower -> spawn
# since plan 12 B5a gave towers a marker kind of their own.
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 12 0
marker 12 0 spawn 3

# ⚠ `rotate` is CUMULATIVE — it turns the ghost, it does not set it.
# From 4, five more turns reach 3; from 3, four more reach 1.
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 6 12
marker 6 12 spawn 2

do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click -12 0
marker -12 0 spawn 0

count markers 4 4
do toggle_mode

# The three corridors.
palette 5
drag 12 0 0 0
drag -12 0 0 0
drag 6 12 0 0

count painted 37 37

# And the seals — one hex across each corridor.
palette 9
click 2 0
click -2 0
click 1 2

kind 2 0 wall
kind -2 0 wall
kind 1 2 wall

# ── The wave: one enemy per corridor ────────────────────────────
wave 3
count alive 3 3
enemies passable

# Nobody is besieging anything yet — they are twelve hexes out and
# walking.  This is the reading that makes the one after it mean
# something: an assertion that were true from the first tick would say
# nothing about the siege.
count targets 0 0
snap f7-the-wave-sets-out

# ── Nine ticks: each enemy is one hex short of its own seal ─────
tick 9
count alive 3 3
enemies passable
enemies distinct
range 3 3

# ⚠ THE PHASE.  Three enemies, three DIFFERENT wall hexes.  One
# nearest-wall target computed for the wave would read 1 here.
count targets 3 3

# ── And it stays a siege: they do not wander, and they do not ───
#    find a way in that is not there.
tick 20
count alive 3 3
enemies passable
enemies distinct
range 3 3
count targets 3 3
